ung.\n\nBut considering my current hand structure, maybe focusing on the pairs and potential chows in the middle suits is better.\n\nAnother angle: Dragon Pung (J2) is worth 2 points, and if I can get another J2, that's good. Since J2 is Green Dragon, and I have two, drawing a third would complete the pung. There are four J2s total, two in my hand, so two left. Possible.\n\nSimilarly, T3: four exist, I have two, so two left.\n\nF2: same.\n\nSo all have some chance.\n\nBut which is more valuable? Completing J2 pung gives Dragon Pung (2 points), whereas T3 pung is just a normal pung (unless part of something else).\n\nSo maybe prioritize J2.\n\nBut I have two J2s; discarding one would break the pair, which is bad. So definitely don't want to discard J2.\n\nSame with T3: have two, so discarding one would leave a single, which is worse than a pair. So better not discard T3 either.\n\nSimilarly, F2: two copies. If I discard one F2, then I have a single F2, which is less useful. But if I think F2 is not helpful (since not seat/prevalent wind), maybe better to discard one to focus on other pairs.\n\nWait, but in Seven Pairs, having multiple pairs is good. Even if F2 pair isn't scoring anything special, it still counts as a pair for Seven Pairs.\n\nSo if aiming for Seven Pairs, keeping all pairs is good. So maybe not discard F2, T3, or J2.\n\nTherefore, the safe discards would be among the single tiles: W2, W3, W5, W9, T8, B4, B7, B9.\n\nNow, among these, which is least useful?\n\nW2 and W3 are close to forming a chow (need W4). So better to keep them.\n\nW5 is a central tile, could fit into many sequences.\n\nW9 is terminal.\n\nT8 is high bamboo.\n\nB4, B7, B9: scattered.\n\nB9 is terminal; maybe discard that.\n\nSimilarly, W9 is terminal.\n\nTerminals are often harder to incorporate into hands unless specifically aiming for terminal-based patterns. Given that I have several honors and middle tiles, maybe discarding a terminal is wise.\n\nComparing B9 and W9: both terminals. Let's see if there's any pattern that uses them.\n\nAll Terminals and Honors is 32 points, but I have too many non-terminals/honors. For example, I have W2, W3, W5 which are simples. So that's out.\n\nOutside Hand (8 points) requires every meld to have terminals or honors. But again, with simples in hand, probably not.\n\nSo terminals might not be contributing much. Thus, discarding one of the terminals.\n\nBetween B9 and W9, does it matter? Maybe check which suit I have more in.\n\nIn dots (B), I have B4, B7, B9. In characters (W), W2, W3, W5, W9. So maybe slightly more characters, but not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider which tile is more dangerous to discard (i.e., opponent might be waiting on it). But without knowledge of others' hands, hard to say.\n\nAnother thought: I have J2 pair (Green Dragon), which is part of Reversible Tiles pattern (12 points). Reversible Tiles requires tiles that are point-symmetric: B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B8, B9, T2, T4, T5, T6, T8, T9, J3. Wait, J2 is Green Dragon, which is not in the list (Reversible Tiles includes J3, White Dragon). So J2 isn't reversible. Hmm, maybe not relevant.\n\nWait, Reversible Tiles example includes J3. So J2 (Green) isn't counted. So that pattern might not apply.\n\nBack to discarding. Let's also consider the \"No Honors\" pattern (1 point), but I have plenty of honors, so not applicable.\n\nEdge Wait (1 point) if winning on a 3 or 7 to complete 123 or 789. But speculative.\n\nPerhaps safest to discard a terminal that's least connected. B9 and W9 are both terminals. Let's see:\n\nIf I keep W9, maybe pair it or use in a pung. But odds are low. Same with B9.\n\nAlternatively, B7: if I keep B7 and B9, could get B8. But I also have B4. Not sure.\n\nWait, another angle: I have two T3s. If I can form a pung of T3, that's good. Additionally, T3 is part of the lower end (1-3), so maybe part of a Lower Tiles hand (24 points), which requires all tiles 1-3. But I have T8, W5, etc., which are higher, so no.\n\nAlterna